    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d215">

                <pb facs="0411" n="349" sameAs="frus:frus1878/pg349"/>

                <head>No. 210.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Evarts to Sir Edward
                    Thornton</hi>.</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1878-06-17"><hi rendition="#italic">June</hi> 17,
                        1878</date>.</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Referring to previous correspondence on the
                    subject, I have now the honor to inform you that the Secretary of the Interior
                    has forwarded to this Department a copy of a recent official report relative to
                    the trade in ammunition alleged to be carried on between Indians belonging to
                    reservations in Montana and Idaho, who cross the frontier, and parties in the
                    British possessions. It is also stated in the report that the traffic <pb
                        facs="0412" n="350" xml:id="pg350"/> in question is very extensive, and that
                    it is said to be encouraged by Her Majesty’s authorities there.</p>
                <p>This information is communicated to you for such action as you may be pleased to
                    take in the premises.</p>
                <p>I have, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>WM. M. EVARTS.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
